{
  "analysis": "Grouped/interleaved approaches are much SLOWER (354-570us vs 291us baseline) — splitting into many small kernel launches hurts (each group launch of (G,S) CTAs underutilizes the GPU: G=1 → only 256 CTAs of 16.8MB... plus per-launch overhead × 32 launches). The eviction policy games don't pay off. The 2-kernel full-grid approach is better. Current best: 0.291ms (27.9x). The kernel-time floor for 2-pass is ~279us; we're at ~287us of kernel time + graph replay overhead.\n\nRemaining ideas: (1) micro-optimize stats/apply (they're already at ~3TB/s); (2) single fused persistent kernel with global barrier — same traffic, maybe saves ~10us; (3) check measurement: replay overhead. Honestly we're near the floor. One more possibility: reduce stats+apply read overlap — none.\n\nBigger question: should I verify with the actual evaluator now, then decide whether to keep optimizing? Run evaluate.py with solution = graph version. First write it as /workspace/solution.py and push it to the box along with evaluate deps. Note: evaluate.py imports from /opt/task (exists on box? /opt/task is in the image — yes, Docker COPY . /opt/task). Run evaluator on the box.",
