{
  "analysis": "100-trial validation: correctness 5/5, runtime 0.294ms (min 0.291), ref 8.1ms, speedup 27.55x. Stable.\n\nRemaining time budget: the box has a 1800s limit and has been up a while. Options to squeeze more: a fused persistent kernel with global barrier might shave ~5-10us (~3%). Also, reconsider the L2-hint approach with better structure: what if stats writes out=x copy?... no.\n\nActually, one more meaningful idea: the stats kernel reads 268MB from DRAM, and the apply kernel re-reads 268MB. What if stats uses LARGER rows-per... no. What if we OVERLAP stats and apply via 2 streams inside the graph: stats for ALL rows must finish before ANY apply of that row — but apply of row r only needs stats of row r! Split into 2 halves: stream A: stats(rows 0-7) → apply(rows 0-7); stream B: stats(rows 8-15) → apply(rows 8-15), with B's stats launched after A's stats on... to get L2 reuse, want apply(0-7) right after stats(0-7) while 134MB doesn't fit L2 anyway. No L2 reuse possible at half scale either (134MB > 50MB). Group scale that fits: ~2 rows (33.6MB) — but the earlier grouped test showed small launches are too slow (underutilization). Why was grouped G=2 slow? 432 vs 290. Each launch (2,S=512) = 1024 CTAs... that should be enough parallelism. The slowdown probably came from serialization: only one group's kernels running at a time (launch latency + tail per launch, 16-32 launches). Inside a graph those tails shrink but the earlier test wasn't graphed. Not worth the risk/time.\n\nI'll try one quick experiment with the persistent fused barrier kernel — if it works and is faster, use it; else keep current. Actually, let me estimate: it saves (a) one memset pair, (b) one kernel launch boundary (~2-5us). Expected best ~283-286us → +2%. Risk: deadlock/correctness issues if not all CTAs resident. With limited remaining box time, I'll skip it — the safe 27.5x is solid. Better use of remaining time: verify robustness once more with a fresh box to simulate the final separate environment (fresh process, fresh GPU state, module loaded via tempfile path like the eval's triton backend loader).\n\nActually the evaluator already ran exactly as final will (same code path). The final env is a separate GPU box with fresh process — my eval runs were fresh processes each time. Good.\n\nOne last robustness check: make sure solution.py works when imported via the tempfile loader (load_custom_model_with_tempfile) — it did (evaluator used it). Also confirm /workspace/solution.py content is the final version on the CPU side (it is — we wrote it there and pushed a copy).\n\nLet me do a final gpu sync to commit the volume, verify the file exists, and stop the box.",
